mentl/9d13315:
Before:
  fn iterate(xs) with Iterate = {
    fn go(i) = if i < xs.len { yield(xs[i]); go(i + 1) } else { () }
    go(0)
  }

The nested `go` closure was a per-call alloc + recursive frame the
mark_tail pass didn't reach through. Each iterate(N-element list)
walked N frames deep in mentl2's emit even when the recursive call
was structurally tail.

After:
  fn iterate(xs) with Iterate = iterate_from(xs, 0, xs.len)
  fn iterate_from(xs, i, n) with Iterate =
    if i < n { yield(xs[i]); iterate_from(xs, i + 1, n) }
    else { () }

Top-level iterate_from. Tail-call-mark recognizes `iterate_from(xs, i+1, n)`
as the tail of the then-branch; emit produces return_call_indirect;
the wasm stack stays bounded.

Note: mentl2 self-compile still traps with deep op_map_h_yield recursion
(observed post-rebuild), indicating the substrate gap is deeper — the
HANDLER-resume continuation chain itself (map_h's yield arm calling
resume() to return into iterate_from's continuation, then iterate_from
calling itself tail) doesn't get fully tail-call-optimized by mentl2's
current emit. The handler-resume-continuation tail-call is named peer
Hβ.emit.handler-resume-continuation-tail-call. This commit closes the
iterate-side gap; the emit-side gap is the next named peer.
